# The project snapshot

A generator can be deterministic only when its input is clear. For Literate, that
input is not one file and not “whatever happens to be on disk.” It is a captured
project snapshot: an ordered collection of documents, the templates they name, and
the settings that govern the interpretation.

The snapshot is the semantic unit shared by generation, publication, inspection,
and language projection. Each host has a different way to capture it, but once
captured the engine receives explicit text rather than hidden filesystem state.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Sources[Discovered literate paths] --> Capture[Capture one project snapshot]
    Buffers[Open editor buffers] --> Capture
    Disk[Saved files and templates] --> Capture
    Capture --> Documents[Ordered source documents]
    Capture --> Templates[Named template text]
    Capture --> Settings[Root project settings]
    Documents --> Engine[Pure project engine]
    Templates --> Engine
    Settings --> Engine
```

The arrows into the snapshot represent acquisition. The arrows out represent
explicit values passed to pure code. The engine does not reach back into the host
to ask whether a file has changed halfway through a calculation.

## One path, one captured text

Every document has a workspace-relative path and its complete text. Paths are
normalized before they become project identities. Absolute paths, attempts to
escape the workspace, control characters, and non-portable collisions are rejected.

The command-line adapter normally captures saved files. The editor can prefer an
open buffer, including unsaved changes, over the same path on disk. This difference
belongs to the host. The engine sees the resulting document in the same form.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Path[One literate path] --> Open{Open in the editor?}
    Open -->|Yes| Buffer[Use captured buffer text]
    Open -->|No| Saved[Use saved file text]
    Buffer --> Snapshot[One document in the snapshot]
    Saved --> Snapshot
```

A snapshot must not combine both texts or switch between them during one operation.
If the buffer changes, the host captures a new snapshot and starts new work.

## Document order is part of meaning

Fragment contributions are ordered, so documents require one deterministic order.
The root `index.literate` comes first. Documents linked from it follow in link
order, and remaining documents follow in normalized path order. Repeated links do
not insert a document twice.

This policy gives an author control over narrative order without turning unlinked
chapters into missing input. It also means index order is more than a table of
contents: moving a chapter can change the order of contributions to a shared
fragment family.

A structural edit must therefore preserve either the relevant document order or
the generated result. Deterministic checks make any unintended consequence visible.

`engine document ordering` turns that policy into a stable rank.

```ts : <<engine document ordering>>=
/** Index first, then linked documents, then remaining paths sorted; preserves input objects. */
export function orderSourceDocuments(documents: SourceDocument[]): SourceDocument[] {
    const paths = new Map<string, number>();
    for (const document of documents) {
        const diagnostic = sourceNulDiagnostic(document);
        if (diagnostic) { throw new Error(`${diagnostic.path}: ${diagnostic.message}`); }
    }
    const canonical = (document: SourceDocument): string => safeRelativePath(document.path) ?? document.path;
    paths.set('index.literate', 0);
    const index = documents.find(document => canonical(document) === 'index.literate');
    if (index) {
        <<engine index link ranks>>
    }
    return [...documents].sort((a, b) => {
        const left = canonical(a), right = canonical(b);
        const rank = (paths.get(left) ?? paths.size) - (paths.get(right) ?? paths.size);
        return rank || (left < right ? -1 : left > right ? 1 : 0);
    });
}

```

## Discover dependencies before loading them

Templates are named by settings and output roots. An adapter needs those names
before it can capture their text, so dependency discovery is deliberately smaller
than generation. It parses only enough structure to return normalized template
paths; it does not expand fragments or pretend that missing template text is valid.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Host
    participant Engine as Dependency discovery
    participant Store as Disk or open buffers

    Host->>Engine: Captured literate documents
    Engine-->>Host: Required template paths
    Host->>Store: Read each required template
    Store-->>Host: Captured template text
    Host->>Engine: Complete documents and templates
```

The host remains responsible for acquisition. The engine remains responsible for
meaning and diagnostics.

## Settings belong to the root

Project-wide settings come from the normalized root document. A similarly named
chapter elsewhere cannot silently take authority. Individual output roots may name
a source template, but they do not redefine unrelated project settings.

Keeping settings in the snapshot prevents global mutable state from leaking between
workspace folders or consecutive commands. Two projects interpreted at the same
time may select different themes or templates without affecting one another.

## Freshness belongs to the caller

A snapshot is a value, not a subscription. The pure engine does not watch files or
cancel itself. An editor or asynchronous adapter records the versions and inputs
used to capture the snapshot, then discards a result if those inputs are no longer
current.

This separation becomes especially important for language servers and publication,
where work may continue after another edit. Later chapters will return to freshness
as a boundary around asynchronous results.

The next chapter treats the snapshot's fragment declarations and references as a
graph. That graph supplies the structure needed for validation and expansion.
